const CONTENT_DATA = {"Concept: Basics of Light and Reflection": {"Subconcept: Reflection Of Light": {"Summary": {"Theory": "What makes the world around us visible? In a dark room, we see nothing. It is only when light falls on an object and bounces back into our eyes that we are able to see it. This bouncing back of light is called **reflection**. \n\nWhen we observe shadows cast by opaque objects, we can conclude that light generally travels in straight lines. This straight-line path is usually indicated as a **ray of light**. However, as shown in the figure below, if an opaque object is extremely small, light tends to bend around it rather than walking in a straight line. This phenomenon is known as **diffraction**. To explain such complex behaviors, modern quantum theory describes light as having both wave and particle properties.\n\nWhen light strikes a highly polished surface, like a mirror, most of it is reflected. This process strictly follows the **laws of reflection**:\n1. The angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection \\( (\\angle i = \\angle r) \\).\n2. The incident ray, the normal to the mirror at the point of incidence, and the reflected ray, all lie in the same plane.\n\nIt is crucial to remember that these laws apply to **all** types of reflecting surfaces, whether they are flat (plane mirrors) or curved (spherical mirrors). \n\nWhen you look at yourself in a plane mirror, the image formed has specific characteristics:\n- It is **virtual and erect** (upright, and cannot be captured on a screen).\n- The size of the image is exactly equal to the size of the object.\n- The image is formed as far behind the mirror as the object is in front of it.\n- It is **laterally inverted** (your left hand appears as the right hand in the mirror).", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C1.S1_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "A text box explaining the diffraction of light and the modern quantum theory that reconciles wave and particle properties.", "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of an image formed by a plane mirror?\n(A) The image is virtual and erect.\n(B) The image is laterally inverted.\n(C) The image is magnified.\n(D) The image is formed at the same distance behind the mirror as the object is in front.", "Answer": "C", "Solution": "An image formed by a plane mirror is always of the same size as the object. It is never magnified (enlarged) or diminished.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "assertion_reason", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Assertion (A): Light always travels in a perfectly straight line, regardless of the size of the opaque object in its path.\nReason (R): The formation of sharp shadows by large opaque objects points to the straight-line propagation of light.\n(A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(C) A is true but R is false.\n(D) A is false but R is true.", "Answer": "D", "Solution": "The Assertion is false. As explained in the figure below, if an opaque object is very small, light tends to bend around it (diffraction), meaning it does not always travel in a perfectly straight line. The Reason is true, as large objects do cast sharp shadows, which is the basis for the straight-line approximation of light in basic optics.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C1.S1_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Text box explaining diffraction and wave-particle duality.", "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "statement_selection", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Consider the following statements regarding the reflection of light:\n1. The laws of reflection are applicable only to plane mirrors and fail for curved surfaces.\n2. The incident ray, the reflected ray, and the normal at the point of incidence all lie in the same plane.\n3. The modern quantum theory of light reconciles its particle properties with its wave nature.\nWhich combination is correct?\n(A) 1 and 2\n(B) 2 and 3\n(C) 1 and 3\n(D) 1, 2 and 3", "Answer": "B", "Solution": "Statement 1 is incorrect because the laws of reflection are applicable to all types of reflecting surfaces, including spherical (curved) mirrors. Statements 2 and 3 are correct based on the laws of reflection and the modern understanding of light's dual nature.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Why are we unable to see anything in a completely dark room, but things become visible when the room is lit up?", "Answer": "We see objects because they reflect light into our eyes; without a light source, there is no light to be reflected.", "Solution": "An object becomes visible only when light falls on it and is reflected back. This reflected light, when received by our eyes, enables us to see the object. In a completely dark room, there is no light to fall on the objects and reflect into our eyes, making them invisible.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "What happens to the straight-line propagation theory of light when an opaque object in its path becomes extremely small? Name the phenomenon.", "Answer": "The straight-line theory fails because light bends around very small objects, a phenomenon known as diffraction.", "Solution": "When an opaque object in the path of light is very small, light has a tendency to bend around it rather than walking in a straight line. This effect is known as the diffraction of light. In such cases, the straight-line treatment of optics using rays fails, and light must be thought of as a wave.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C1.S1_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Text box explaining diffraction.", "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "numerical", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A ray of light strikes a highly polished plane mirror such that the angle between the incident ray and the surface of the mirror is \\( 35^\\circ \\). Calculate the angle of reflection.", "Answer": "\\( 55^\\circ \\)", "Solution": "1. Given: Angle between the incident ray and the mirror surface = \\( 35^\\circ \\).\n2. Formula: The angle of incidence \\( (\\angle i) \\) is measured between the incident ray and the normal (which is perpendicular to the mirror). Therefore, \\( \\angle i = 90^\\circ - \\text{surface angle} \\). The law of reflection states \\( \\angle i = \\angle r \\).\n3. Substitution: \\( \\angle i = 90^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 55^\\circ \\).\n4. Answer: Since \\( \\angle r = \\angle i \\), the angle of reflection \\( \\angle r = 55^\\circ \\).\n5. Interpretation: The light ray reflects at an angle of \\( 55^\\circ \\) relative to the normal.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A student stands 3 meters in front of a large plane mirror. What is the total distance between the student and their image? State the property of the plane mirror used to find this.", "Solution": "1. Given: Distance of the student (object) in front of the mirror = 3 m.\n2. Find: Total distance between the student and the image.\n3. Concept: One of the properties of an image formed by a plane mirror is that the image is formed as far behind the mirror as the object is in front of it.\n4. Step-by-step: \n   - Image distance behind the mirror = 3 m.\n   - Total distance = Distance of student to mirror + Distance of mirror to image.\n   - Total distance = 3 m + 3 m = 6 m.\n5. Answer: The total distance between the student and their image is 6 m.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A light ray is incident on a plane mirror. If the total angle between the incident ray and the reflected ray is \\( 110^\\circ \\), what is the angle of incidence?", "Solution": "1. Given: The angle between the incident ray and the reflected ray is \\( 110^\\circ \\). This means \\( \\angle i + \\angle r = 110^\\circ \\).\n2. Find: The angle of incidence \\( (\\angle i) \\).\n3. Formula: According to the first law of reflection, the angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection \\( (\\angle i = \\angle r) \\).\n4. Substitute: \n   \\[ \\angle i + \\angle i = 110^\\circ \\]\n   \\[ 2\\angle i = 110^\\circ \\]\n   \\[ \\angle i = \\frac{110^\\circ}{2} = 55^\\circ \\]\n5. Answer: The angle of incidence is \\( 55^\\circ \\).\n6. Interpret: The normal bisects the total angle between the incident and reflected rays perfectly in half.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}}, "Concept: Spherical Mirrors": {"Subconcept: Spherical Mirrors - Introduction": {"Summary": {"Theory": "When light falls on a highly polished surface like a mirror, most of it is reflected. You already know the laws of reflection: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, and the incident ray, normal, and reflected ray all lie in the same plane. These laws apply to all reflecting surfaces, including curved ones.\n\nWhile a plane mirror always forms a virtual, erect image of the same size as the object, curved mirrors behave differently. The most common curved mirrors are **spherical mirrors**, whose reflecting surfaces form a part of a hollow sphere. As shown in the figure below, there are two main types:\n1. **Concave mirror**: The reflecting surface is curved inwards, facing the centre of the sphere (like the inner bowl of a spoon).\n2. **Convex mirror**: The reflecting surface is curved outwards (like the back of a spoon).\n\nTo understand how these mirrors form images, we must define a few key terms:\n- **Pole (P)**: The centre of the reflecting surface of the mirror. It lies exactly on the mirror's surface.\n- **Centre of curvature (C)**: The centre of the imaginary sphere of which the mirror is a part. It lies *outside* the mirror surface. For a concave mirror, it is in front; for a convex mirror, it is behind the mirror.\n- **Radius of curvature (\\(R\\))**: The radius of that imaginary sphere. The distance from the pole to the centre of curvature is equal to \\(R\\).\n- **Principal axis**: An imaginary straight line passing through the pole and the centre of curvature. It is perpendicular (normal) to the mirror at its pole.\n- **Aperture**: The diameter of the circular outline of the mirror's reflecting surface.\n\nWhen rays of light parallel to the principal axis strike a spherical mirror, they reflect in a specific way (see see the figure below). In a concave mirror, they actually intersect at a single point on the principal axis called the **principal focus (F)**. In a convex mirror, the reflected rays diverge, but they *appear* to come from a point behind the mirror, which is its principal focus. \n\nThe distance between the pole and the principal focus is the **focal length (\\(f\\))**. For spherical mirrors with small apertures, the radius of curvature is exactly twice the focal length. This gives us the important relationship:\n\\[ R = 2f \\]\nThis means the principal focus lies exactly midway between the pole and the centre of curvature.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C2.S1_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Schematic representation of concave and convex mirrors showing the shaded non-reflecting side.", "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Where does the centre of curvature lie for a convex mirror?\n(A) In front of the reflecting surface\n(B) Behind the reflecting surface\n(C) Exactly on the reflecting surface\n(D) At the principal focus", "Answer": "B", "Solution": "The reflecting surface of a convex mirror curves outwards. Therefore, the centre of the imaginary sphere of which it forms a part lies behind the mirror.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "statement_selection", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Consider the following statements about spherical mirrors:\n1. The pole lies on the surface of the mirror.\n2. The centre of curvature is a part of the reflecting surface.\n3. The principal axis is normal to the mirror at its pole.\nWhich combination is correct?\n(A) 1 and 2\n(B) 2 and 3\n(C) 1 and 3\n(D) 1, 2 and 3", "Answer": "C", "Solution": "Statement 1 is correct; the pole is the centre of the reflecting surface itself. Statement 2 is incorrect; the centre of curvature is the centre of the imaginary sphere and lies outside the reflecting surface. Statement 3 is correct; the principal axis passes through the pole and centre of curvature, making it normal to the mirror at the pole.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "assertion_reason", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Assertion (A): For a spherical mirror of small aperture, the principal focus lies exactly midway between the pole and the centre of curvature.\nReason (R): The radius of curvature is equal to twice the focal length (\\( R = 2f \\)) for mirrors of small aperture.\n(A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(C) A is true but R is false.\n(D) A is false but R is true.", "Answer": "A", "Solution": "Both statements are true. The distance from the pole to the centre of curvature is \\(R\\), and the distance from the pole to the principal focus is \\(f\\). Because \\(R = 2f\\), the focal length is exactly half the radius of curvature, meaning the principal focus is physically located at the midpoint between the pole and the centre of curvature.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "How does the principal focus of a concave mirror differ from that of a convex mirror? Explain with reference to the behaviour of parallel light rays.", "Answer": "The principal focus of a concave mirror is real and lies in front of it, whereas for a convex mirror, it is virtual and lies behind it.", "Solution": "When light rays parallel to the principal axis strike a concave mirror, they reflect and actually converge at a specific point in front of the mirror. This point is a real principal focus. However, when parallel rays strike a convex mirror, they reflect and diverge. These diverging rays appear to come from a point behind the mirror, making the principal focus of a convex mirror virtual.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C2.S1_2", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Ray diagrams showing parallel rays converging for a concave mirror and diverging for a convex mirror.", "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "During an experiment, a student directs the reflecting surface of a concave mirror towards the Sun and holds a sheet of paper near it. After a few minutes, the paper begins to burn. Give the scientific reason for this observation.", "Answer": "The concave mirror converges the parallel rays of sunlight to a single point (the principal focus), concentrating heat energy that ignites the paper.", "Solution": "Light rays coming from the Sun are parallel to each other. When these parallel rays fall on a concave mirror, the mirror converges them to a single sharp point on its principal axis, known as the principal focus. The concentration of sunlight at this single point generates a large amount of heat energy, which is sufficient to ignite the paper.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Define the principal focus of a concave mirror.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}, "Question 4": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "The radius of curvature of a spherical mirror is 20 cm . What is its focal length?", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}, "Question 5": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Find the focal length of a convex mirror whose radius of curvature is 32 cm .", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "The focal length of a convex mirror used as a rear-view mirror in a car is 1.5 m. Calculate its radius of curvature.", "Solution": "1. **Given:** Focal length of the convex mirror, \\( f = 1.5 \\text{ m} \\).\n2. **Find:** Radius of curvature, \\( R \\).\n3. **Formula:** For spherical mirrors of small aperture, the relationship between radius of curvature and focal length is \\( R = 2f \\).\n4. **Substitute:** \\( R = 2 \\times 1.5 \\text{ m} \\).\n5. **Answer:** \\( R = 3.0 \\text{ m} \\).\n6. **Interpret:** The radius of the imaginary sphere of which this mirror is a part is 3.0 metres.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "You are given a highly polished, curved metal spoon. When you look at its inner curved surface, you can see your image. What type of spherical mirror does this inner surface approximate, and where does its centre of curvature lie relative to the reflecting surface?", "Solution": "1. **Identify the surface:** The inner curved surface of a spoon is curved inwards, facing the centre of its imaginary sphere.\n2. **Match with mirror type:** A spherical mirror whose reflecting surface is curved inwards is called a concave mirror.\n3. **Determine centre of curvature position:** By definition, the centre of curvature of a concave mirror lies in front of its reflecting surface.\n4. **Conclusion:** The inner surface approximates a concave mirror, and its centre of curvature lies in front of it.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 3": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "A student observes that a spherical mirror has a radius of curvature of 40 cm. If the aperture of the mirror is small, at what distance from the pole will a beam of light parallel to the principal axis converge or appear to diverge?", "Solution": "1. **Given:** Radius of curvature, \\( R = 40 \\text{ cm} \\).\n2. **Find:** The distance from the pole where parallel rays converge or appear to diverge.\n3. **Concept:** The point where rays parallel to the principal axis converge or appear to diverge is called the principal focus. The distance from the pole to the principal focus is the focal length (\\( f \\)).\n4. **Formula:** \\( f = \\frac{R}{2} \\).\n5. **Substitute:** \\( f = \\frac{40 \\text{ cm}}{2} \\).\n6. **Answer:** \\( f = 20 \\text{ cm} \\).\n7. **Interpret:** The parallel beam of light will converge (if it is a concave mirror) or appear to diverge (if it is a convex mirror) at a distance of 20 cm from the pole of the mirror.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}, "Subconcept: Image Formation by Spherical Mirrors": {"Summary": {"Theory": "When we place an object in front of a spherical mirror, the mirror forms an image. But how do we know where this image will be, how big it will be, or whether it will be real or virtual? \n\nTo find out, we can use **ray diagrams**. An extended object acts like a collection of infinite point sources, each emitting infinite rays. For clarity, we only need to choose **two specific rays** whose paths after reflection are easy to predict. The intersection of these two reflected rays gives us the position of the image.\n\nHere are the four standard rays you can choose from:\n1. A ray parallel to the principal axis, after reflection, will pass through the **principal focus** \\(F\\) (in a concave mirror) or appear to diverge from it (in a convex mirror).\n2. A ray passing through the principal focus \\(F\\), after reflection, will emerge parallel to the principal axis.\n3. A ray passing through the **centre of curvature** \\(C\\), after reflection, is reflected back along the exact same path. This happens because a line from the centre of curvature acts as a normal to the spherical surface.\n4. A ray incident obliquely to the principal axis at the **pole** \\(P\\) is reflected obliquely, following the laws of reflection (angle of incidence equals angle of reflection).\n\nBy applying these rules, we can determine the nature of images formed by a concave mirror for different object positions:\n- **At infinity:** Image is at \\(F\\), highly diminished (point-sized), real and inverted.\n- **Beyond \\(C\\):** Image is between \\(F\\) and \\(C\\), diminished, real and inverted.\n- **At \\(C\\):** Image is at \\(C\\), same size as the object, real and inverted.\n- **Between \\(C\\) and \\(F\\):** Image is beyond \\(C\\), enlarged, real and inverted.\n- **Between \\(P\\) and \\(F\\):** Image is formed behind the mirror, enlarged, virtual and erect.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A student wants to find the approximate focal length of a concave mirror using sunlight. Describe the setup and explain how the focal length is determined. What is the nature of the image formed on the paper?", "Answer": "Focus sunlight onto a sheet of paper to get a sharp bright spot; the distance from the mirror to the paper is the focal length. The image is real, inverted, and point-sized.", "Solution": "1. Hold the concave mirror so its reflecting surface is directed towards the Sun.\n2. Direct the reflected light onto a sheet of paper held close to the mirror.\n3. Move the paper back and forth until a sharp, bright spot of light is obtained. This spot is the actual image of the Sun.\n4. Measure the distance between the mirror and the paper. This distance gives the approximate focal length of the mirror.\n5. Because the Sun is at infinity, the image formed at the focus is real, inverted, and highly diminished (point-sized).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "diagram", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "To construct a ray diagram for a spherical mirror, we typically choose two specific rays whose paths are easy to trace. State any two such rays and describe their path after reflection from a concave mirror.", "Answer": "1. A ray parallel to the principal axis passes through the principal focus. 2. A ray passing through the centre of curvature reflects back along the same path.", "Solution": "To locate an image, any two of the following rays can be considered:\n1. A ray parallel to the principal axis, after reflection, will pass through the principal focus \\(F\\).\n2. A ray passing through the principal focus \\(F\\), after reflection, will emerge parallel to the principal axis.\n3. A ray passing through the centre of curvature \\(C\\), after reflection, is reflected back along the same path (because it falls normally on the mirror surface).\n4. A ray incident obliquely to the principal axis at the pole \\(P\\) is reflected obliquely, making equal angles with the principal axis.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A student places a burning candle exactly at the centre of curvature \\(C\\) of a concave mirror. Describe the position, size, and nature of the image formed. How will these characteristics change if the candle is moved slightly towards the principal focus \\(F\\)?", "Answer": "At \\(C\\), the image is at \\(C\\), same size, real and inverted. If moved between \\(C\\) and \\(F\\), the image moves beyond \\(C\\), becomes enlarged, but remains real and inverted.", "Solution": "When the object is placed exactly at the centre of curvature \\(C\\), the image is also formed at \\(C\\). The image is of the same size as the object, and its nature is real and inverted.\n\nWhen the object is moved slightly towards the principal focus (so it lies between \\(C\\) and \\(F\\)), the image shifts further away from the mirror (beyond \\(C\\)). The size of the image becomes enlarged, while its nature remains real and inverted.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 4": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "The image formed by a concave mirror is observed to be virtual, erect and larger than the object. Where should be the position of the object?", "Answer": "d", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 5": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Name a mirror that can give an erect and enlarged image of an object.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A concave mirror forms a real, inverted, and diminished image of an object. Based on the rules of image formation, where must the object be placed, and where will the image be located?", "Solution": "1. Given: The image formed by a concave mirror is real, inverted, and diminished.\n2. Recall: A highly diminished (point-sized) image is formed when the object is at infinity. A diminished (but not point-sized) image is formed when the object is placed beyond the centre of curvature \\(C\\).\n3. Answer: The object must be placed beyond the centre of curvature \\(C\\).\n4. Image Position: For an object placed beyond \\(C\\), the reflected rays intersect between the principal focus \\(F\\) and the centre of curvature \\(C\\). Therefore, the image is located between \\(F\\) and \\(C\\).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An object is placed between the pole \\(P\\) and the principal focus \\(F\\) of a concave mirror. Using the standard rules for drawing ray diagrams, explain step-by-step how to locate the image. State the nature and size of the resulting image.", "Solution": "1. Object Position: The object is placed between the pole \\(P\\) and the principal focus \\(F\\).\n2. Ray 1: Consider a ray from the top of the object parallel to the principal axis. After reflection, this ray passes through the principal focus \\(F\\).\n3. Ray 2: Consider a second ray from the top of the object passing through the centre of curvature \\(C\\). It strikes the mirror normally and reflects back along the same path.\n4. Intersection: The two reflected rays diverge in front of the mirror, meaning they will never meet to form a real image. \n5. Virtual Intersection: When these diverging rays are produced backwards behind the mirror, they appear to intersect at a point.\n6. Conclusion: The point of intersection behind the mirror gives the position of the image. Because the rays do not actually meet, the image is virtual and erect. The image is also enlarged compared to the object.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}, "Subconcept: Representation of Images Formed by Spherical Mirrors Using Ray Diagrams": {"Summary": {"Theory": "To construct ray diagrams for spherical mirrors, we consider the intersection of at least two reflected rays to locate the image of a point object. For clarity, we choose two rays whose directions after reflection are easily known:\n\n1. A ray parallel to the principal axis, after reflection, passes through the principal focus \\( F \\) (concave mirror) or appears to diverge from it (convex mirror).\n2. A ray passing through or directed towards the principal focus \\( F \\), after reflection, emerges parallel to the principal axis.\n3. A ray passing through or directed towards the centre of curvature \\( C \\), after reflection, retraces its path because it falls normally on the mirror surface.\n4. A ray incident obliquely to the principal axis at the pole \\( P \\) is reflected obliquely, following the laws of reflection (angle of incidence equals angle of reflection).\n\n**Image Formation by Concave Mirrors:**\nThe nature, position, and size of the image depend on the object's position relative to \\( P \\), \\( F \\), and \\( C \\). \n- Object at infinity: Image at \\( F \\) (real, point-sized).\n- Object beyond \\( C \\): Image between \\( F \\) and \\( C \\) (real, diminished).\n- Object at \\( C \\): Image at \\( C \\) (real, same size).\n- Object between \\( C \\) and \\( F \\): Image beyond \\( C \\) (real, enlarged).\n- Object at \\( F \\): Image at infinity (real, highly enlarged).\n- Object between \\( P \\) and \\( F \\): Image behind the mirror (virtual, erect, enlarged).\n\n**Uses of Concave Mirrors:** Used in torches, headlights (to get parallel beams), shaving mirrors, and by dentists to see enlarged images. Large concave mirrors concentrate sunlight in solar furnaces.\n\n**Image Formation by Convex Mirrors:**\nA convex mirror always forms a virtual, erect, and diminished image, regardless of the object's position.\n- Object at infinity: Image at \\( F \\) behind the mirror (point-sized).\n- Object between infinity and \\( P \\): Image between \\( P \\) and \\( F \\) behind the mirror (diminished).\n\n**Uses of Convex Mirrors:** Preferred as rear-view mirrors in vehicles because they always give an erect image and have a wider field of view due to their outward curvature.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C2.S3_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Consider a ray of light directed towards the principal focus of a convex mirror. According to the rules for drawing ray diagrams, how will this ray travel after reflection?\n(A) It will reflect back along the same path.\n(B) It will emerge parallel to the principal axis.\n(C) It will pass through the centre of curvature.\n(D) It will reflect obliquely, making an equal angle with the principal axis.", "Answer": "B", "Solution": "According to the rules for ray diagrams, a ray passing through the principal focus of a concave mirror or a ray which is directed towards the principal focus of a convex mirror, after reflection, will emerge parallel to the principal axis. This is illustrated in the source text and corresponding figures.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C2.S3_2", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "diagram", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Describe the ray diagram used to locate the image of an object placed exactly at the centre of curvature \\( C \\) of a concave mirror. State the nature and size of the image formed.", "Answer": "The image is real, inverted, and of the same size as the object, formed exactly at \\( C \\).", "Solution": "1. Draw a concave mirror with pole \\( P \\), principal focus \\( F \\), and centre of curvature \\( C \\).\n2. Place the object \\( AB \\) vertically at \\( C \\).\n3. Draw a first ray from the top of the object \\( A \\) parallel to the principal axis. After reflection, it passes through the focus \\( F \\).\n4. Draw a second ray from \\( A \\) passing through the focus \\( F \\). After reflection, it emerges parallel to the principal axis.\n5. The two reflected rays intersect at point \\( A' \\), which lies exactly below \\( C \\).\n6. The resulting image \\( A'B' \\) is formed at \\( C \\). It is real, inverted, and exactly the same size as the object.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C2.S3_5", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "diagram", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An object is placed at a finite distance in front of a convex mirror. Describe the two standard rays used to construct its image. Where do these rays intersect, and what is the nature of the image?", "Answer": "The image is virtual, erect, and diminished, formed between the pole \\( P \\) and focus \\( F \\) behind the mirror.", "Solution": "1. Draw a convex mirror with its pole \\( P \\), and mark its focus \\( F \\) and centre of curvature \\( C \\) behind the mirror.\n2. Place an object \\( AB \\) at a finite distance in front of the mirror.\n3. First ray: Draw a ray from the top of the object \\( A \\) parallel to the principal axis. It reflects such that it appears to diverge from the focus \\( F \\) behind the mirror.\n4. Second ray: Draw a ray from \\( A \\) directed towards the centre of curvature \\( C \\). It reflects back along its own path.\n5. The reflected rays diverge in front of the mirror. When extended backwards (using dotted lines), they intersect at a point \\( A' \\) between \\( P \\) and \\( F \\) behind the mirror.\n6. This intersection forms an image \\( A'B' \\) that is virtual, erect, and diminished in size.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C2.S3_6", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A dentist uses a small mirror to examine a patient's teeth. Identify the type of mirror used and explain, based on its image formation properties, why it is suitable for this purpose.", "Answer": "A concave mirror is used because it forms an enlarged, virtual, and erect image when the object is placed very close to it.", "Solution": "Dentists use concave mirrors. When a concave mirror is held very close to the teeth (such that the teeth lie between the pole \\( P \\) and the principal focus \\( F \\) of the mirror), it forms a virtual, erect, and enlarged image. This magnified view helps the dentist examine the teeth clearly and in an upright orientation.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 4": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "No matter how far you stand from a mirror, your image appears erect. The mirror is likely to be", "Answer": "['i: -0.18 m', 'ii: +0.67 m']", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 5": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "We wish to obtain an erect image of an object, using a concave mirror of focal length 15 cm . What should be the range of distance of the object from the mirror? What is the nature of the image? Is the image larger or smaller than the object? Draw a ray diagram to show the image formation in this case.", "Answer": "Convex lens; +3.0 D", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 6": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Name the type of mirror used in the following situations.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 7": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Why do we prefer a convex mirror as a rear-view mirror in vehicles?", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Explain how the ray diagram for an object at infinity helps in determining the approximate focal length of a concave mirror.", "Solution": "1. **Identify the Object Position:** When an object is at infinity (such as the Sun), the rays of light coming from it are parallel to each other and to the principal axis.\n2. **Apply Ray Rules:** According to the rules of reflection for a concave mirror, rays parallel to the principal axis converge at a single point on the principal axis after reflection.\n3. **Locate the Image:** This point of convergence is the principal focus \\( F \\) of the mirror. The image formed is real, inverted, and point-sized.\n4. **Determine Focal Length:** By placing a sheet of paper (screen) at this point of convergence to obtain a sharp, bright spot, the distance between the mirror's pole \\( P \\) and the screen gives the approximate focal length \\( f \\) of the mirror.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C2.S3_5", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Why does a convex mirror provide a wider field of view compared to a plane mirror of the same size? Explain using the concept of image formation.", "Solution": "1. **Surface Curvature:** A convex mirror has a reflecting surface that is curved outwards.\n2. **Divergence of Rays:** When parallel rays from a wide area fall on a convex mirror, they are reflected outwards (diverged). Because the normal to the surface at any point points outwards, rays from a much larger angular range can be reflected into the observer's eye.\n3. **Image Characteristics:** A convex mirror always forms a highly diminished, virtual, and erect image of an object regardless of its distance.\n4. **Conclusion:** By shrinking the apparent size of objects, a convex mirror can fit more objects into the same mirror area, thereby providing a much wider field of view than a plane mirror, which forms same-sized images.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 3": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "A student observes that a spherical mirror forms a real, inverted, and highly enlarged image of a candle flame on a screen. Identify the type of mirror and the specific position of the candle flame. Describe the ray diagram that supports this observation.", "Solution": "1. **Identify the Mirror:** Only a concave mirror can form a real image on a screen. A convex mirror always forms virtual images.\n2. **Identify the Position:** According to the image formation table for a concave mirror, a real, inverted, and highly enlarged image is formed at infinity when the object is placed exactly at the principal focus \\( F \\).\n3. **Describe the Ray Diagram:** \n   - Place the object at \\( F \\).\n   - Draw a ray parallel to the principal axis; after reflection, it passes through \\( F \\).\n   - Draw a second ray directed towards the pole \\( P \\) obliquely; it reflects obliquely, making an equal angle with the principal axis.\n4. **Interpret the Diagram:** The two reflected rays become parallel to each other. Parallel rays do not meet at a finite distance; they are considered to meet at infinity, thus forming a highly enlarged, real, and inverted image.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C2.S3_5", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}, "Subconcept: Sign Convention for Reflection by Spherical Mirrors": {"Summary": {"Theory": "While dealing with the reflection of light by spherical mirrors, we follow a specific set of rules called the **New Cartesian Sign Convention**. This system helps us consistently assign positive or negative values to distances, which is crucial for solving numerical problems.\n\nIn this convention, we imagine the mirror placed on a standard coordinate graph:\n1. The **pole** (\\(P\\)) of the mirror is taken as the **origin** (0,0).\n2. The **principal axis** of the mirror is taken as the **x-axis** (\\(X'X\\)) of the coordinate system.\n\nThe rules for measuring distances are as follows:\n- **Object Position:** The object is always placed to the left of the mirror. This means light from the object falls on the mirror from the left-hand side.\n- **Horizontal Distances:** All distances parallel to the principal axis are measured from the pole of the mirror. Distances measured to the right of the origin (along the +x-axis) are taken as **positive**, while those measured to the left of the origin (along the -x-axis) are taken as **negative**.\n- **Vertical Distances:** Distances measured perpendicular to and above the principal axis (along the +y-axis) are taken as **positive**. Distances measured perpendicular to and below the principal axis (along the -y-axis) are taken as **negative**.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": true, "image_description": "Diagram showing the New Cartesian Sign Convention for spherical mirrors.", "generation_description": "A clear coordinate geometry diagram featuring a concave mirror at the center. The pole (P) is marked at the origin (0,0). The principal axis is the horizontal x-axis. An object is drawn as an upward arrow on the left side (-x axis). Arrows indicate: 'Direction of incident light' (left to right), 'Distance towards the left (-ve)', 'Distance towards the right (+ve)', 'Height upwards (+ve)' (pointing up from the axis), and 'Height downwards (-ve)' (pointing down from the axis)."}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "According to the New Cartesian Sign Convention, what are the standard signs for the object distance and the height of an object placed upright in front of a spherical mirror?\n(A) Object distance is positive, height is positive.\n(B) Object distance is negative, height is positive.\n(C) Object distance is negative, height is negative.\n(D) Object distance is positive, height is negative.", "Answer": "B", "Solution": "According to the convention, the object is always placed to the left of the mirror. Since distances measured to the left of the pole (origin) are negative, the object distance is always negative. An upright object is placed above the principal axis, and distances measured above the principal axis are taken as positive. Therefore, the object distance is negative and the height is positive.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "assertion_reason", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Assertion (A): The object distance for any spherical mirror is always taken as a negative value.\nReason (R): The object is always placed to the left of the mirror, and all distances measured to the left of the pole are taken as negative.\n(A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(C) A is true but R is false.\n(D) A is false but R is true.", "Answer": "A", "Solution": "In the New Cartesian Sign Convention, the pole of the mirror is the origin. The convention dictates that the object is always placed to the left of the mirror, meaning light travels from left to right. Because all distances parallel to the principal axis are measured from the pole, any distance to the left (along the -x-axis) is negative. Thus, both the assertion and the reason are true, and the reason correctly explains the assertion.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "In the New Cartesian Sign Convention for spherical mirrors, which point on the mirror is chosen as the origin, and which line represents the x-axis of the coordinate system?", "Answer": "The pole of the mirror is chosen as the origin, and the principal axis represents the x-axis.", "Solution": "According to the New Cartesian Sign Convention, the coordinate system is set up such that the **pole** (\\(P\\)) of the spherical mirror acts as the origin (0,0). The **principal axis** of the mirror is taken as the x-axis (\\(X'X\\)) along which all horizontal distances are measured.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An image formed by a spherical mirror is measured to have a negative height. What does this negative sign indicate about the orientation of the image according to the New Cartesian Sign Convention? Explain why.", "Answer": "A negative height indicates that the image is inverted, meaning it is formed below the principal axis.", "Solution": "In the New Cartesian Sign Convention, vertical distances are measured relative to the principal axis (which acts as the x-axis). Distances measured perpendicular to and above the principal axis (along the +y-axis) are taken as positive. Conversely, distances measured perpendicular to and below the principal axis (along the -y-axis) are taken as negative. Therefore, a negative height means the image extends below the principal axis, which corresponds to an inverted image.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A student places a candle flame \\(15 \\text{ cm}\\) to the left of a concave mirror. The mirror forms an inverted image of the flame \\(30 \\text{ cm}\\) in front of the mirror. The flame is \\(2 \\text{ cm}\\) tall, and the image is \\(4 \\text{ cm}\\) tall. Assign the correct signs to the object distance (\\(u\\)), image distance (\\(v\\)), object height (\\(h\\)), and image height (\\(h'\\)) using the New Cartesian Sign Convention.", "Solution": "1. **Given:** Object is \\(15 \\text{ cm}\\) to the left, image is \\(30 \\text{ cm}\\) in front (left), object is upright (\\(2 \\text{ cm}\\)), image is inverted (\\(4 \\text{ cm}\\)).\n2. **Object distance (\\(u\\)):** The object is placed to the left of the pole. Distances to the left are negative. So, \\(u = -15 \\text{ cm}\\).\n3. **Image distance (\\(v\\)):** The image is formed in front of the mirror, which is to the left of the pole. Thus, \\(v = -30 \\text{ cm}\\).\n4. **Object height (\\(h\\)):** The candle flame is placed upright, above the principal axis. Distances above the axis are positive. So, \\(h = +2 \\text{ cm}\\).\n5. **Image height (\\(h'\\)):** The image is inverted, meaning it forms below the principal axis. Distances below the axis are negative. So, \\(h' = -4 \\text{ cm}\\).\n6. **Interpretation:** The assigned signs correctly reflect the positions (left/right) and orientations (above/below) relative to the pole and principal axis.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}, "Subconcept: Mirror Formula and Magnification": {"Summary": {"Theory": "In a spherical mirror, the distance of the object from its pole is called the **object distance** (\\(u\\)). The distance of the image from the pole of the mirror is called the **image distance** (\\(v\\)). You already know that the distance of the principal focus from the pole is called the **focal length** (\\(f\\)).\n\nThere is a relationship between these three quantities given by the **mirror formula** which is expressed as:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f} \\]\nThis formula is valid in all situations for all spherical mirrors for all positions of the object. You must use the New Cartesian Sign Convention (as illustrated in see the figure below) while substituting numerical values for \\(u\\), \\(v\\), \\(f\\), and \\(R\\) in the mirror formula for solving problems.\n\n**Magnification**\nMagnification produced by a spherical mirror gives the relative extent to which the image of an object is magnified with respect to the object size. It is usually represented by the letter \\(m\\). If \\(h\\) is the height of the object and \\(h'\\) is the height of the image, then the magnification \\(m\\) is given by:\n\\[ m = \\frac{\\text{Height of the image } (h')}{\\text{Height of the object } (h)} = \\frac{h'}{h} \\]\n\nThe magnification \\(m\\) is also related to the object distance (\\(u\\)) and image distance (\\(v\\)). It can be expressed as:\n\\[ m = \\frac{h'}{h} = -\\frac{v}{u} \\]\n\n**Sign Convention for Magnification:**\n- The height of the object is taken to be positive as the object is usually placed above the principal axis.\n- The height of the image should be taken as positive for virtual images.\n- The height of the image is to be taken as negative for real images.\n- A **negative sign** in the value of the magnification indicates that the image is **real**.\n- A **positive sign** in the value of the magnification indicates that the image is **virtual**.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C2.S5_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Diagram showing the New Cartesian Sign Convention for spherical mirrors, indicating positive and negative directions for distances and heights.", "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "If the magnification produced by a spherical mirror is \\(-0.5\\), what can be concluded about the nature of the image?\n(A) The image is virtual and erect.\n(B) The image is real and inverted.\n(C) The image is virtual and inverted.\n(D) The image is real and erect.", "Answer": "B", "Solution": "According to the sign convention, a negative sign in the value of the magnification indicates that the image is real. Real images formed by spherical mirrors are always inverted.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "numerical", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An object is placed at a distance of \\(20 \\text{ cm}\\) in front of a concave mirror of focal length \\(10 \\text{ cm}\\). Calculate the image distance and the magnification produced.", "Answer": "\\(v = -20 \\text{ cm}\\), \\(m = -1\\)", "Solution": "1. Given: Object distance \\(u = -20 \\text{ cm}\\), Focal length \\(f = -10 \\text{ cm}\\) (since it is a concave mirror).\n2. Formula: \\[ \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f} \\] and \\[ m = -\\frac{v}{u} \\]\n3. Substitution:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{-20} = \\frac{1}{-10} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{v} = -\\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{20} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{-2 + 1}{20} = -\\frac{1}{20} \\]\nSo, \\(v = -20 \\text{ cm}\\).\nFor magnification:\n\\[ m = -\\frac{-20}{-20} = -1 \\]\n4. Answer: The image distance is \\(-20 \\text{ cm}\\) and magnification is \\(-1\\).\n5. Interpretation: The image is formed \\(20 \\text{ cm}\\) in front of the mirror. The negative magnification indicates the image is real, inverted, and of the exact same size as the object.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A student observes that the magnification produced by a spherical mirror is \\(+2.5\\). What does the positive sign and the value \\(2.5\\) signify regarding the nature and size of the image?", "Answer": "The positive sign indicates a virtual and erect image, while the value \\(2.5\\) means the image is enlarged to \\(2.5\\) times the height of the object.", "Solution": "According to the sign convention, the height of the object is taken as positive. Since magnification \\(m = \\frac{h'}{h}\\), a positive magnification means the height of the image (\\(h'\\)) is also positive. This corresponds to a virtual and erect image. The numerical value \\(2.5\\) indicates that the image is enlarged, specifically \\(2.5\\) times the height of the object.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An object is placed at a distance of 10 cm from a convex mirror of focal length 15 cm . Find the position and nature of the image.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 4": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "The magnification produced by a plane mirror is +1 . What does this mean?", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 5": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An object 5.0 cm in length is placed at a distance of 20 cm in front of a convex mirror of radius of curvature 30 cm . Find the position of the image, its nature and size.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 6": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An object of size 7.0 cm is placed at 27 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 18 cm . At what distance from the mirror should a screen be placed, so that a sharp focussed image can be obtained? Find the size and the nature of the image.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 7": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A concave mirror produces three times magnified (enlarged) real image of an object placed at 10 cm in front of it. Where is the image located?", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "A concave mirror produces a real image that is twice the size of the object. If the focal length of the mirror is \\(15 \\text{ cm}\\), find the object distance.", "Solution": "1. Given: Focal length \\(f = -15 \\text{ cm}\\) (concave mirror). The image is real and twice the size of the object, so magnification \\(m = -2\\).\n2. Find: Object distance (\\(u\\)).\n3. Formula: \\(m = -\\frac{v}{u}\\) and \\[ \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f} \\]\n4. Substitute:\nFrom the magnification formula:\n\\[ -2 = -\\frac{v}{u} \\]\n\\[ v = 2u \\]\nSubstitute \\(v = 2u\\) into the mirror formula:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{2u} + \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{-15} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1 + 2}{2u} = -\\frac{1}{15} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{3}{2u} = -\\frac{1}{15} \\]\n\\[ 2u = -45 \\]\n\\[ u = -22.5 \\text{ cm} \\]\n5. Answer: The object distance is \\(-22.5 \\text{ cm}\\).\n6. Interpret: The object must be placed \\(22.5 \\text{ cm}\\) in front of the concave mirror to obtain a real image twice its size.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": true, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A convex mirror used for rear-view on an automobile has a radius of curvature of 3.00 m. If a bus is located at 5.00 m from this mirror, find the position, nature and size of the image.", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "ncert"}, "Example 3": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": true, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An object, 4.0 cm in size, is placed at 25.0 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 15.0 cm. At what distance from the mirror should a screen be placed in order to obtain a sharp image? Find the nature and the size of the image.", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "ncert"}}}}, "Concept: Refraction Of Light": {"Subconcept: Refraction Of Light - Introduction": {"Summary": {"Theory": "Light seems to travel along straight-line paths as long as it remains in a single transparent medium. However, when light enters from one transparent medium to another, it does not always move along a straight-line path. \n\nWhen light travels **obliquely** (at an angle) from one transparent medium to another, the direction of propagation of light in the second medium changes. This phenomenon is known as the **refraction of light**.\n\nBecause of refraction, the light reaching our eyes from an object placed in a different medium seems to come from a different direction. This leads to several common optical illusions in our day-to-day life:\n\n* The bottom of a tank or a pond containing water appears to be raised.\n* When a thick glass slab is placed over printed matter, the letters appear raised when viewed from the top.\n* A pencil partly immersed in water appears to be displaced or bent at the interface of air and water.\n* A lemon kept in water in a glass tumbler appears to be bigger than its actual size when viewed from the sides.\n* A coin hidden from view in an empty bowl becomes visible again when water is poured into it, because the coin appears slightly raised above its actual position.\n\nThe extent of this bending effect is not the same everywhere; it is different for different pairs of media. For example, a pencil will not appear displaced to the exact same extent if we use liquids like kerosene or turpentine instead of water.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A lemon kept in a glass tumbler containing water appears to be bigger than its actual size when viewed from the sides. Which phenomenon is primarily responsible for this observation?\n(A) Reflection of light\n(B) Refraction of light\n(C) Dispersion of light\n(D) Scattering of light", "Answer": "B", "Solution": "The apparent increase in the size of the lemon is due to the refraction of light. When light rays travel from the water into the air, they change their direction. This bending of light makes the object appear larger than it actually is.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "statement_selection", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Consider the following statements regarding a pencil partly immersed in a transparent liquid:\n1. The pencil appears displaced at the interface of air and the liquid.\n2. The extent of apparent displacement of the pencil is exactly the same whether the liquid is water, kerosene, or turpentine.\n3. The apparent displacement occurs because light from the underwater portion of the pencil seems to come from a different direction.\nWhich combination is correct?\n(A) 1 and 2\n(B) 2 and 3\n(C) 1 and 3\n(D) 1, 2 and 3", "Answer": "C", "Solution": "Statement 1 is correct as the pencil appears bent at the interface. Statement 3 is correct because refraction causes the light reaching our eyes to seem to come from a different direction. Statement 2 is incorrect because the extent of the bending effect is different for different pairs of media; water, kerosene, and turpentine will all displace the pencil to different extents.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "assertion_reason", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Assertion (A): When a glass slab is placed normal (perpendicular) to a straight line drawn on a paper, the part of the line under the glass slab does not appear bent from the sides.\nReason (R): The direction of propagation of light changes only when it travels obliquely from one transparent medium to another.\n(A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(C) A is true but R is false.\n(D) A is false but R is true.", "Answer": "A", "Solution": "Both statements are true. Refraction (the bending of light) occurs when light enters a new medium obliquely (at an angle). If the glass slab is placed normal to the line, the light rays travel perpendicular to the interface and do not change direction. Therefore, the line does not appear bent, making R the correct explanation for A.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "You place a coin in a shallow bowl and move away until it just disappears from your sight. When a friend gently pours water into the bowl, the coin becomes visible again. Explain why this happens.", "Answer": "The coin becomes visible again because pouring water into the bowl causes the light rays from the coin to refract, making the coin appear slightly raised above its actual position.", "Solution": "When the bowl is empty, the light rays from the coin travel in a straight line and are blocked by the opaque edge of the bowl from reaching your eye. When water is poured into the bowl, the light rays travelling from the water into the air change their direction (refract) at the interface. Because of this bending, the light reaching your eye seems to come from a higher point. This makes the coin appear slightly raised above its actual position, allowing it to clear the edge of the bowl and become visible to you again.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "If a pencil partly immersed in water appears displaced at the air-water interface, will it appear displaced to the exact same extent if immersed in kerosene instead of water? Justify your answer.", "Answer": "No, the pencil will not appear displaced to the same extent because the extent of refraction is different for different pairs of media.", "Solution": "The apparent displacement of the pencil is caused by the refraction of light as it travels from the liquid into the air. The extent to which light bends depends on the specific pair of media it is travelling through. Since water and kerosene are different transparent media, light will change its direction by a different amount in each case. Therefore, the pencil will appear displaced to a different extent in kerosene compared to water.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A ray of light travelling in air enters obliquely into water. Does the light ray bend towards the normal or away from the normal? Why?", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A student observes that the bottom of a water tank appears to be raised. What optical phenomenon is responsible for this, and what exactly happens to the light rays to cause this effect?", "Solution": "1. Identify the phenomenon: The phenomenon responsible is the refraction of light.\n2. Explain the mechanism: When light rays travel from the water (inside the tank) to the air (outside), they cross the interface of two different transparent media. As they do so, they change their direction of propagation.\n3. Relate to the observation: Because the light rays bend as they enter the air, the rays reaching the observer's eyes seem to originate from a point higher than the actual bottom of the tank. This optical illusion makes the bottom appear raised.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A thick straight line is drawn on a sheet of white paper. A rectangular glass slab is placed over the line. In Case 1, the edge of the slab makes an angle with the line. In Case 2, the edge of the slab is placed exactly normal (perpendicular) to the line. Describe how the line will appear when viewed from the sides in both cases, and state the reason for any difference.", "Solution": "1. Analyze Case 1 (Slab at an angle): The light rays from the line travel obliquely from the glass slab into the air. Because light travelling obliquely from one medium to another changes direction (refracts), the part of the line under the slab will appear bent at the edges.\n2. Analyze Case 2 (Slab normal to the line): The light rays from the line hit the glass-air interface perpendicularly (normally). Light incident normally does not change its direction of propagation.\n3. Conclusion: In Case 1, the line appears bent due to refraction. In Case 2, the line does not appear bent because refraction only causes an apparent bending of objects when light travels obliquely across the interface of two different transparent media.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}, "Subconcept: Refraction through a Rectangular Glass Slab": {"Summary": {"Theory": "When light travels obliquely from one transparent medium to another, it changes its direction. This phenomenon is called **refraction**. Refraction occurs due to a change in the speed of light as it enters from one transparent medium to another.\n\nAs illustrated in the figure below, when a ray of light passes through a rectangular glass slab, it undergoes refraction at two opposite interfaces:\n1. **Air-Glass Interface (Rarer to Denser):** The light ray (incident ray EO) enters from air to glass at point O. Since it moves from a rarer to a denser medium, it slows down and bends **towards the normal**.\n2. **Glass-Air Interface (Denser to Rarer):** The refracted ray (OO') travels through the glass and exits at point O'. Here, it moves from a denser to a rarer medium, speeds up, and bends **away from the normal** to become the emergent ray (O'H).\n\nYou will observe that the emergent ray is parallel to the direction of the incident ray. This happens because the extent of bending of the ray of light at the opposite parallel faces (air-glass and glass-air interfaces) of the rectangular glass slab is equal and opposite. However, the light ray is shifted sideward slightly. This sideward shift is called lateral displacement.\n\n**Laws of Refraction of Light:**\nExperiments show that refraction of light occurs according to the following laws:\n(i) The incident ray, the refracted ray, and the normal to the interface of two transparent media at the point of incidence, all lie in the same plane.\n(ii) **Snell's law of refraction:** The ratio of the sine of the angle of incidence to the sine of the angle of refraction is a constant, for the light of a given colour and for the given pair of media (for angle \\( 0 < i < 90^\\circ \\)).\nIf \\( i \\) is the angle of incidence and \\( r \\) is the angle of refraction, then:\n\\[ \\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r} = \\text{constant} \\]\nThis constant value is called the refractive index of the second medium with respect to the first.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C3.S2_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Diagram showing the refraction of light through a rectangular glass slab, illustrating the incident ray, refracted ray, emergent ray, and lateral displacement.", "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "What happens to a ray of light when it travels obliquely from air into a rectangular glass slab?\n(A) It bends away from the normal.\n(B) It bends towards the normal.\n(C) It travels undeviated in a straight line.\n(D) It reflects completely back into the air.", "Answer": "B", "Solution": "When a light ray enters from a rarer medium (air) to a denser medium (glass), its speed decreases, causing it to bend towards the normal.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "assertion_reason", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Assertion (A): When a light ray passes through a rectangular glass slab, the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray.\nReason (R): The extent of bending of the ray of light at the opposite parallel faces of the rectangular glass slab is equal and opposite.\n(A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(C) A is true but R is false.\n(D) A is false but R is true.", "Answer": "A", "Solution": "The emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray because the bending towards the normal at the first air-glass interface is exactly cancelled out by the bending away from the normal at the second glass-air interface. Therefore, both the assertion and reason are true, and the reason correctly explains the assertion.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "statement_selection", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Consider the following statements regarding the refraction of light:\n1. Refraction is caused by the change in the speed of light as it enters from one transparent medium to another.\n2. According to Snell's law, the ratio of the angle of incidence to the angle of refraction is constant.\n3. The incident ray, the refracted ray, and the normal at the point of incidence all lie in the same plane.\nWhich combination is correct?\n(A) 1 and 2\n(B) 2 and 3\n(C) 1 and 3\n(D) 1, 2 and 3", "Answer": "C", "Solution": "Statement 1 is correct; refraction is due to a change in the speed of light. Statement 3 is the first law of refraction and is correct. Statement 2 is incorrect because Snell's law states that the ratio of the *sine* of the angle of incidence to the *sine* of the angle of refraction is constant (\\( \\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r} = \\text{constant} \\)), not the ratio of the angles themselves.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "State the fundamental cause of refraction of light when it travels from one transparent medium to another.", "Answer": "Refraction is caused by the change in the speed of light as it enters from one transparent medium to another.", "Solution": "Light travels at different speeds in different transparent media. When a light ray enters obliquely from one medium to another, its speed changes. This change in speed causes the light ray to bend or change its direction of propagation, which is known as refraction.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "When a light ray passes through a rectangular glass slab, the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray but slightly shifted sideways. Explain why the emergent ray emerges parallel to the incident ray.", "Answer": "The emergent ray is parallel because the extent of bending at the two opposite parallel faces of the glass slab is equal and opposite.", "Solution": "When light enters from air to glass, it bends towards the normal by a certain extent. When it exits from glass to air at the opposite parallel face, it bends away from the normal by the exact same extent. Because the two refracting faces of the rectangular glass slab are parallel to each other, the equal and opposite bending cancels out any angular deviation. As a result, the emergent ray is parallel to the direction of the incident ray.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "numerical", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A ray of light is incident on a transparent glass surface at an angle of incidence of \\( 60^\\circ \\). If the angle of refraction is \\( 30^\\circ \\), calculate the constant value for this pair of media using Snell's law.", "Answer": "\\( \\sqrt{3} \\) or 1.732", "Solution": "1. Given: Angle of incidence \\( i = 60^\\circ \\), Angle of refraction \\( r = 30^\\circ \\).\n2. Formula: According to Snell's law, \\( \\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r} = \\text{constant} \\).\n3. Substitution: \\( \\frac{\\sin 60^\\circ}{\\sin 30^\\circ} \\).\n4. Calculation: We know \\( \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) and \\( \\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\). Substituting these values gives \\( \\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\sqrt{3} \\).\n5. Interpretation: The constant value (which represents the refractive index of the glass with respect to air) is \\( \\sqrt{3} \\) or approximately 1.732.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Based on the experiment of refraction through a rectangular glass slab, describe the behavior of the light ray at the two interfaces (air-glass and glass-air).", "Solution": "Step 1: At the first interface (air to glass), the light ray enters from a rarer medium (air) to a denser medium (glass). Because its speed decreases, it bends **towards** the normal.\nStep 2: At the second interface (glass to air), the light ray enters from a denser medium (glass) to a rarer medium (air). Because its speed increases, it bends **away from** the normal.\nStep 3: The overall effect is that the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray but laterally shifted.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C3.S2_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Diagram showing refraction of light through a rectangular glass slab, highlighting the incident, refracted, and emergent rays.", "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "For a given pair of media, the constant in Snell's law is 1.5. If a light ray strikes the interface with an angle of incidence of \\( 45^\\circ \\), find the sine of the angle of refraction.", "Solution": "1. Given: Constant \\( = 1.5 \\), Angle of incidence \\( i = 45^\\circ \\).\n2. Find: \\( \\sin r \\) (sine of the angle of refraction).\n3. Formula: Snell's law states \\( \\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r} = \\text{constant} \\).\n4. Substitute: \\( \\frac{\\sin 45^\\circ}{\\sin r} = 1.5 \\). We know that \\( \\sin 45^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 0.707 \\).\n5. Calculate: \\( \\frac{0.707}{\\sin r} = 1.5 \\), which gives \\( \\sin r = \\frac{0.707}{1.5} \\approx 0.471 \\).\n6. Interpret: The sine of the angle of refraction for this light ray is approximately 0.471.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}, "Subconcept: The Refractive Index": {"Summary": {"Theory": "When a ray of light travels obliquely from one transparent medium into another, it changes its direction. This bending happens because light propagates with different speeds in different media. Light travels fastest in a vacuum with a speed of \\( c = 3 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\). In air, the speed is only marginally less, but it reduces considerably in media like water or glass.\n\nThe extent of the change in direction is expressed by the **refractive index**. \n\n**Relative Refractive Index:**\nConsider light travelling from medium 1 to medium 2, as shown in the figure below. Let \\( v_1 \\) be the speed of light in medium 1 and \\( v_2 \\) be the speed of light in medium 2. The refractive index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1 (denoted as \\( n_{21} \\)) is the ratio of the speed of light in medium 1 to the speed of light in medium 2:\n\\[ n_{21} = \\frac{\\text{Speed of light in medium 1}}{\\text{Speed of light in medium 2}} = \\frac{v_1}{v_2} \\]\nSimilarly, the refractive index of medium 1 with respect to medium 2 is:\n\\[ n_{12} = \\frac{v_2}{v_1} \\]\n\n**Absolute Refractive Index:**\nIf medium 1 is a vacuum or air, the refractive index of medium 2 is called its **absolute refractive index** (or simply, refractive index), denoted by \\( n_m \\). If \\( c \\) is the speed of light in air/vacuum and \\( v \\) is the speed of light in the medium:\n\\[ n_m = \\frac{\\text{Speed of light in air}}{\\text{Speed of light in the medium}} = \\frac{c}{v} \\]\nFor example, as seen in the figure below, the refractive index of water is 1.33, meaning the ratio of the speed of light in air to that in water is 1.33.\n\n**Optical Density vs. Mass Density:**\nThe ability of a medium to refract light is expressed in terms of its **optical density**. In comparing two media, the one with the larger refractive index is the **optically denser medium**, and light travels slower in it. The medium with the lower refractive index is the **optically rarer medium**, where light travels faster. \n\nIt is important to note that optical density is not the same as mass density. An optically denser medium may not have a greater mass density. For instance, kerosene has a higher refractive index (1.44) than water (1.33), making it optically denser, even though its mass density is lower than that of water.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C3.S3_2", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Ray diagram showing light travelling from medium 1 (air) to medium 2 (glass), bending towards the normal.", "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "assertion_reason", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Assertion (A): Kerosene is optically denser than water, even though its mass density is less than that of water.\nReason (R): The speed of light in kerosene is less than the speed of light in water.\n(A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(C) A is true but R is false.\n(D) A is false but R is true.", "Answer": "B", "Solution": "Assertion (A) is true as kerosene has a higher refractive index (1.44) than water (1.33), making it optically denser despite having a lower mass density. Reason (R) is also true because light travels slower in an optically denser medium. However, R is just a consequence of kerosene being optically denser; it does not explain *why* kerosene's mass density is lower. Mass density and optical density are two completely different physical properties. Therefore, both are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "numerical", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "The speed of light in a transparent plastic is \\( 2.0 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\). Calculate its absolute refractive index. Take the speed of light in vacuum as \\( 3 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\).", "Answer": "1.5", "Solution": "1. Given:\nSpeed of light in vacuum, \\( c = 3 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)\nSpeed of light in plastic, \\( v = 2.0 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)\n\n2. Formula:\nAbsolute refractive index, \\( n_m = \\frac{c}{v} \\)\n\n3. Substitution:\n\\[ n_m = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1}}{2.0 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1}} \\]\n\\[ n_m = 1.5 \\]\n\n4. Answer:\nThe absolute refractive index of the plastic is 1.5. (Note: Refractive index is a ratio of two similar quantities and has no units).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "numerical", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "The refractive index of medium A with respect to medium B is 1.2. If the speed of light in medium A is \\( 2.0 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\), calculate the speed of light in medium B.", "Answer": "\\( 2.4 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)", "Solution": "1. Given:\nRefractive index of medium A with respect to medium B, \\( n_{AB} = 1.2 \\)\nSpeed of light in medium A, \\( v_A = 2.0 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)\n\n2. Formula:\nThe refractive index of medium A with respect to medium B is the ratio of the speed of light in medium B to the speed of light in medium A.\n\\[ n_{AB} = \\frac{v_B}{v_A} \\]\n\n3. Substitution:\n\\[ 1.2 = \\frac{v_B}{2.0 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1}} \\]\n\\[ v_B = 1.2 \\times 2.0 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\]\n\\[ v_B = 2.4 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\]\n\n4. Answer:\nThe speed of light in medium B is \\( 2.4 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Explain the difference between an optically denser medium and an optically rarer medium in terms of the speed of light.", "Answer": "In an optically denser medium, the speed of light is lower, whereas in an optically rarer medium, the speed of light is higher.", "Solution": "When comparing two transparent media, the one with the larger refractive index is called the optically denser medium, and the one with the lower refractive index is the optically rarer medium. Because refractive index is inversely proportional to the speed of light in that medium (\\( n = \\frac{c}{v} \\)), a higher refractive index means a lower speed of light. Therefore, light travels slower in an optically denser medium and faster in an optically rarer medium.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 4": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Light enters from air to glass having refractive index 1.50. What is the speed of light in the glass? The speed of light in vacuum is $3 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{~m} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}, "Question 5": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Find out, from Table 9.3, the medium having highest optical density. Also find the medium with lowest optical density.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}, "Question 6": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "You are given kerosene, turpentine and water. In which of these does the light travel fastest? Use the information given in Table 9.3.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}, "Question 7": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "The refractive index of diamond is 2.42 . What is the meaning of this statement?", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Light travels through a special liquid at a speed of \\( 2.25 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\). Find the absolute refractive index of this liquid. (Speed of light in vacuum = \\( 3 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)).", "Solution": "1. Given:\nSpeed of light in vacuum, \\( c = 3 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)\nSpeed of light in the liquid, \\( v = 2.25 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)\n\n2. Find:\nAbsolute refractive index of the liquid, \\( n_m \\)\n\n3. Formula:\n\\[ n_m = \\frac{c}{v} \\]\n\n4. Substitute:\n\\[ n_m = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{2.25 \\times 10^8} \\]\n\\[ n_m = \\frac{3}{2.25} = \\frac{300}{225} = 1.33 \\]\n\n5. Answer:\nThe absolute refractive index of the liquid is 1.33.\n\n6. Interpret:\nThis value is identical to the refractive index of water, meaning light travels at the same speed in this liquid as it does in water.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "The absolute refractive index of diamond is 2.42. Calculate the speed of light in diamond. (Speed of light in vacuum \\( c = 3 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)).", "Solution": "1. Given:\nAbsolute refractive index of diamond, \\( n_d = 2.42 \\)\nSpeed of light in vacuum, \\( c = 3 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)\n\n2. Find:\nSpeed of light in diamond, \\( v_d \\)\n\n3. Formula:\n\\[ n_d = \\frac{c}{v_d} \\]\nRearranging for \\( v_d \\):\n\\[ v_d = \\frac{c}{n_d} \\]\n\n4. Substitute:\n\\[ v_d = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{2.42} \\]\n\\[ v_d \\approx 1.24 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\]\n\n5. Answer:\nThe speed of light in diamond is approximately \\( 1.24 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\).\n\n6. Interpret:\nBecause diamond has a very high refractive index (it is highly optically dense), the speed of light reduces significantly when travelling through it compared to air or vacuum.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 3": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "A ray of light travels from medium 1 to medium 2. The speed of light in medium 1 is \\( 2.5 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\) and in medium 2 is \\( 2.0 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\). Calculate the refractive index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1 (\\( n_{21} \\)) and the refractive index of medium 1 with respect to medium 2 (\\( n_{12} \\)). What is the relationship between \\( n_{21} \\) and \\( n_{12} \\)?", "Solution": "1. Given:\nSpeed of light in medium 1, \\( v_1 = 2.5 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)\nSpeed of light in medium 2, \\( v_2 = 2.0 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\)\n\n2. Find:\n\\( n_{21} \\) and \\( n_{12} \\)\n\n3. Formula:\n\\[ n_{21} = \\frac{v_1}{v_2} \\]\n\\[ n_{12} = \\frac{v_2}{v_1} \\]\n\n4. Substitute:\nFor \\( n_{21} \\):\n\\[ n_{21} = \\frac{2.5 \\times 10^8}{2.0 \\times 10^8} = \\frac{2.5}{2.0} = 1.25 \\]\n\nFor \\( n_{12} \\):\n\\[ n_{12} = \\frac{2.0 \\times 10^8}{2.5 \\times 10^8} = \\frac{2.0}{2.5} = 0.8 \\]\n\n5. Answer:\nThe refractive index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1 is 1.25. \nThe refractive index of medium 1 with respect to medium 2 is 0.8.\n\n6. Interpret:\nNotice that \\( 1.25 \\times 0.8 = 1 \\). This shows that \\( n_{21} = \\frac{1}{n_{12}} \\). The relative refractive indices of two media are reciprocals of each other.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}, "Subconcept: Refraction by Spherical Lenses": {"Summary": {"Theory": "Have you ever looked through a magnifying glass or touched the lenses in someone's spectacles? You might have noticed they are curved. A transparent material bound by two surfaces, of which at least one is spherical, forms a **lens**. \n\nThere are two main types of spherical lenses:\n1. **Convex Lens (Double Convex):** It is thicker in the middle and thinner at the edges. As shown in the figure below (a), when parallel rays of light pass through it, they bend inwards and meet at a point. Because it brings light rays together, it is called a **converging lens**.\n2. **Concave Lens (Double Concave):** It is thicker at the edges and thinner in the middle. As shown in the figure below (b), when parallel rays pass through it, they spread out. Because it spreads light rays apart, it is called a **diverging lens**.\n\nTo understand lenses, we need to know a few important terms:\n- **Centres of Curvature (\\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\)):** A lens has two spherical surfaces, and each surface is part of a sphere. The centres of these spheres are the centres of curvature. Since there are two surfaces, a lens has two centres of curvature.\n- **Principal Axis:** An imaginary straight line passing through the two centres of curvature.\n- **Optical Centre (\\(O\\)):** The central point of the lens. A ray of light passing through the optical centre goes straight through without suffering any deviation.\n- **Aperture:** The effective diameter of the circular outline of the lens.\n\n**Principal Focus and Focal Length:**\nIf you hold a convex lens towards the Sun and focus the light on a piece of paper, the paper will eventually burn. This is because the parallel rays of sunlight are converged by the lens to a single sharp, bright spot. This spot is the **principal focus** of the convex lens. The concentrated heat energy at this point burns the paper.\n\nFor a concave lens, parallel rays of light appear to diverge from a specific point on the principal axis after refraction. This point is the principal focus of the concave lens. Because light can pass through a lens from either side, every lens has two principal foci, represented as \\(F_1\\) and \\(F_2\\). The distance from the optical centre (\\(O\\)) to the principal focus is called the **focal length** (\\(f\\)).", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C3.S4_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Diagram showing the converging action of a convex lens and the diverging action of a concave lens.", "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "assertion_reason", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Assertion (A): A double convex lens is also known as a converging lens.\nReason (R): A double convex lens is thicker at the edges than at the middle and converges light rays falling on it.\n(A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(C) A is true but R is false.\n(D) A is false but R is true.", "Answer": "C", "Solution": "The Assertion is true because a convex lens converges light rays, hence it is called a converging lens. However, the Reason is false because a convex lens is thicker at the middle as compared to the edges, not thicker at the edges. A lens thicker at the edges is a concave lens.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "statement_selection", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Consider the following statements regarding the principal focus of spherical lenses:\n1. For a convex lens, rays parallel to the principal axis actually converge at the principal focus after refraction.\n2. For a concave lens, rays parallel to the principal axis appear to diverge from the principal focus after refraction.\n3. A spherical lens has only one principal focus because light can only travel in one direction.\nWhich combination is correct?\n(A) 1 and 2\n(B) 2 and 3\n(C) 1 and 3\n(D) 1, 2 and 3", "Answer": "A", "Solution": "Statements 1 and 2 are correct descriptions of the principal focus for convex and concave lenses, respectively, as shown in the figure below. Statement 3 is incorrect because light can pass through a transparent lens from either side, meaning a lens has two principal foci (\\(F_1\\) and \\(F_2\\)).", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C3.S4_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Shows parallel rays converging at F2 for a convex lens, and diverging from F1 for a concave lens.", "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "If you hold a convex lens directed towards the Sun and focus the light on a sheet of paper, the paper eventually catches fire. Explain the reason behind this observation.", "Answer": "The convex lens converges the parallel rays of sunlight to a single point (the principal focus), concentrating heat energy that causes the paper to burn.", "Solution": "The light from the Sun constitutes parallel rays of light. When these rays pass through a convex lens, the lens converges them to a sharp, bright spot on the paper. This bright spot is actually a real image of the Sun formed at the principal focus of the lens. The concentration of sunlight at this single point generates a significant amount of heat, which causes the paper to burn.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Differentiate between a convex lens and a concave lens based on their physical shape and their effect on parallel rays of light.", "Answer": "A convex lens is thicker in the middle and converges parallel light rays, whereas a concave lens is thicker at the edges and diverges parallel light rays.", "Solution": "1. Physical Shape: A convex lens is thicker at the middle as compared to its edges. A concave lens is thicker at the edges than at the middle.\n2. Effect on Light: When parallel rays of light pass through a convex lens, they bend inwards and converge at a point on the principal axis (converging action). When parallel rays pass through a concave lens, they bend outwards and appear to diverge from a point on the principal axis (diverging action).", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C3.S4_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "Diagram showing converging action of convex lens and diverging action of concave lens.", "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Which one of the following materials cannot be used to make a lens?", "Answer": "d", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A student finds a lens in the science laboratory. When she touches it, she feels that it is thicker at the edges and thinner in the middle. When she passes parallel rays of light through it, they spread out. Identify the type of lens and define its principal focus.", "Solution": "1. Identify the shape: The lens is thicker at the edges and thinner in the middle. This is the physical characteristic of a double concave lens.\n2. Identify the optical action: The lens spreads out parallel rays of light. This is known as diverging action, which confirms it is a concave lens.\n3. Define principal focus: For a concave lens, when rays of light parallel to the principal axis fall on it, they refract and appear to diverge from a specific point on the principal axis. This point is called the principal focus of the concave lens.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Unlike a spherical mirror which has only one centre of curvature and one principal focus, a spherical lens has two centres of curvature (\\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\)) and two principal foci (\\(F_1\\) and \\(F_2\\)). Explain why this is the case.", "Solution": "1. Centres of Curvature: A spherical lens is bound by two transparent surfaces, and at least one (usually both in double lenses) is spherical. Each of these spherical surfaces forms a part of a separate sphere. Since there are two spheres involved in forming the two surfaces of the lens, there are two centres of curvature (\\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\)).\n2. Principal Foci: A lens is a transparent medium, which means light can pass through it from either side (left to right, or right to left). If parallel rays are passed from the left surface, they converge/diverge at a focus on the right. If parallel rays are passed from the right surface, they converge/diverge at a focus on the left. Therefore, a lens has two principal foci (\\(F_1\\) and \\(F_2\\)).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}, "Subconcept: Image Formation by Lenses": {"Summary": {"Theory": "Lenses form images by refracting light. To understand how these images are formed, we use ray diagrams. We can locate the image of an object by considering the intersection of at least two specific refracted rays. For both convex and concave lenses, we typically choose from three standard rays:\n\n1. A ray parallel to the principal axis: After refraction from a convex lens, it passes through the principal focus (\\(F_2\\)) on the other side. For a concave lens, it appears to diverge from the principal focus (\\(F_1\\)) on the same side.\n2. A ray passing through the principal focus: For a convex lens, a ray passing through \\(F_1\\) emerges parallel to the principal axis. For a concave lens, a ray directed towards \\(F_2\\) emerges parallel to the principal axis.\n3. A ray passing through the optical centre (\\(O\\)): This ray emerges without any deviation for both types of lenses.\n\n**Image Formation by a Convex Lens:**\nA convex lens generally forms real and inverted images. As the object moves closer to the lens from infinity, the real image moves further away from the lens and grows in size. \n- When the object is beyond \\(2F_1\\), the image is diminished and between \\(F_2\\) and \\(2F_2\\).\n- When the object is exactly at \\(2F_1\\), the image is of the same size and exactly at \\(2F_2\\).\n- When the object is between \\(F_1\\) and \\(2F_1\\), the image is enlarged and beyond \\(2F_2\\).\n- A special case occurs when the object is placed very close to the lens, between the principal focus (\\(F_1\\)) and the optical centre (\\(O\\)). Here, the refracted rays diverge, forming a **virtual, erect, and enlarged** image on the same side as the object.\n\n**Image Formation by a Concave Lens:**\nA concave lens behaves differently. Because it is a diverging lens, the refracted rays always spread out. They never physically meet to form a real image. Instead, they appear to diverge from a point on the same side as the object. Therefore, a concave lens **always** forms a virtual, erect, and diminished image, regardless of where the object is placed.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A ray of light is directed towards the optical centre (\\(O\\)) of a convex lens. How will this ray behave after passing through the lens?\n(A) It will emerge parallel to the principal axis.\n(B) It will pass through the principal focus on the other side.\n(C) It will emerge without suffering any deviation.\n(D) It will be reflected back along the same path.", "Answer": "C", "Solution": "According to the rules for drawing ray diagrams for spherical lenses, a ray of light passing through the optical centre of a lens (both convex and concave) emerges without any deviation.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "statement_selection", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Consider the following statements regarding the image formed by a concave lens:\n1. The image is always virtual and erect.\n2. The image can be larger than the object if the object is placed very close to the lens.\n3. For an object at a finite distance, the image is always formed between the optical centre and the principal focus.\nWhich combination of statements is correct?\n(A) 1 and 2\n(B) 2 and 3\n(C) 1 and 3\n(D) 1, 2 and 3", "Answer": "C", "Solution": "Statement 1 is correct; a concave lens always forms a virtual and erect image. Statement 2 is incorrect; a concave lens always forms a diminished (smaller) image, never an enlarged one. Statement 3 is correct; for any finite object distance, the virtual image is formed between the principal focus (\\(F_1\\)) and the optical centre (\\(O\\)). Therefore, 1 and 3 are correct.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "assertion_reason", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Assertion (A): A convex lens can form both real and virtual images depending on the position of the object.\nReason (R): When an object is placed between the principal focus (\\(F_1\\)) and the optical centre (\\(O\\)) of a convex lens, the refracted rays diverge and appear to meet on the same side of the lens.\n(A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(C) A is true but R is false.\n(D) A is false but R is true.", "Answer": "A", "Solution": "A convex lens generally forms real images when the object is placed beyond its principal focus. However, when the object is placed very close to the lens (between \\(F_1\\) and \\(O\\)), the refracted rays diverge. When extended backwards, they form a virtual, erect, and enlarged image on the same side. Thus, the reason correctly explains why a convex lens is capable of forming a virtual image, making both statements true and R the correct explanation of A.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "diagram", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Describe the ray diagram used to locate the image of an object placed exactly at \\(2F_1\\) in front of a convex lens. State the position and relative size of the image formed.", "Answer": "The image is formed at \\(2F_2\\) on the other side of the lens and is of the same size as the object.", "Solution": "1. Draw a convex lens with its principal axis. Mark the optical centre \\(O\\), and points \\(F_1\\) and \\(2F_1\\) on the left, and \\(F_2\\) and \\(2F_2\\) on the right.\n2. Draw the object exactly at \\(2F_1\\).\n3. Draw a ray from the top of the object parallel to the principal axis. After refraction, it passes through \\(F_2\\).\n4. Draw a second ray from the top of the object passing through the optical centre \\(O\\) without deviation.\n5. The two refracted rays intersect exactly at \\(2F_2\\) on the right side. The image formed is real, inverted, and exactly the same size as the object.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "diagram", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A ray of light parallel to the principal axis is incident on a convex lens and a concave lens separately. Describe how the ray behaves after refraction in both cases to illustrate their converging and diverging actions.", "Answer": "In a convex lens, the ray converges to pass through the principal focus on the other side. In a concave lens, it diverges and appears to come from the principal focus on the same side.", "Solution": "For a convex lens, the incident ray parallel to the principal axis bends towards the principal axis after refraction and passes exactly through the principal focus (\\(F_2\\)) on the opposite side. This demonstrates its converging action.\nFor a concave lens, the incident parallel ray bends away from the principal axis after refraction. When this divergent emergent ray is extended backwards with a dotted line, it appears to originate from the principal focus (\\(F_1\\)) located on the same side as the incident ray, demonstrating its diverging action.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "diagram", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Both a convex lens and a concave lens can form a virtual and erect image. Describe the difference in the relative size of the virtual image formed by these two lenses, and state where the object must be placed in a convex lens to achieve this.", "Answer": "A convex lens forms an enlarged virtual image when the object is between \\(F_1\\) and \\(O\\), while a concave lens always forms a diminished virtual image for any object position.", "Solution": "1. In a convex lens, to obtain a virtual and erect image, the object must be placed very close to the lens, specifically between the principal focus (\\(F_1\\)) and the optical centre (\\(O\\)). The refracted rays diverge, and when produced backwards, they meet on the same side as the object, forming an image that is enlarged (larger than the object).\n2. In a concave lens, the image is always virtual and erect regardless of where the object is placed. However, the refracted rays diverge in such a way that their backward extensions always intersect between the principal focus (\\(F_1\\)) and the optical centre (\\(O\\)), resulting in an image that is always diminished (smaller than the object).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 4": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Explain why a concave lens always forms a virtual, erect, and diminished image irrespective of the position of the object.", "Answer": "A concave lens diverges incident light rays, so the refracted rays never actually meet but only appear to diverge from a point on the same side as the object.", "Solution": "A concave lens is a diverging lens. When rays of light from an object pass through it, they bend away from the principal axis. Because the refracted rays spread out (diverge) continuously, they cannot physically intersect on the other side of the lens to form a real image. To an observer looking through the lens, these diverging rays appear to come from a point behind the lens (on the same side as the object). This apparent intersection creates an image that is always virtual, erect, and smaller than the object (diminished).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 5": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Where should an object be placed in front of a convex lens to get a real image of the size of the object?", "Answer": "b", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A student observes that a particular spherical lens always produces an image that is smaller than the object, erect, and virtual, no matter where the object is placed in front of it. Identify the type of lens and state where the image is formed when the object is placed at infinity.", "Solution": "1. **Given:** The image is always diminished, erect, and virtual for all object positions.\n2. **Identify the lens:** According to the properties of spherical lenses, a convex lens can form both real and virtual images (which can be enlarged or diminished), but a concave lens *always* forms a virtual, erect, and diminished image. Therefore, the lens is a concave lens.\n3. **Image position at infinity:** For a concave lens, when the object is at infinity, the parallel rays of light diverge after refraction. When extended backwards, they appear to meet at the principal focus (\\(F_1\\)) on the same side as the object.\n4. **Answer:** The lens is a concave lens, and the image is formed at the principal focus (\\(F_1\\)).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An object is placed between the principal focus (\\(F_1\\)) and twice the focal length (\\(2F_1\\)) of a convex lens. Describe the step-by-step construction of the ray diagram to locate the image, and state the nature and relative size of the image formed.", "Solution": "1. **Setup:** Draw a convex lens with its principal axis. Mark the optical centre \\(O\\), and points \\(F_1\\) and \\(2F_1\\) on the left, and \\(F_2\\) and \\(2F_2\\) on the right.\n2. **Place Object:** Draw the object (an arrow pointing upwards) between \\(F_1\\) and \\(2F_1\\).\n3. **Ray 1:** Draw a ray from the top of the object parallel to the principal axis. After refraction, this ray passes through the principal focus \\(F_2\\) on the other side.\n4. **Ray 2:** Draw a second ray from the top of the object passing straight through the optical centre \\(O\\) without any deviation.\n5. **Locate Image:** Extend both refracted rays until they intersect. The point of intersection will lie beyond \\(2F_2\\) on the right side of the lens.\n6. **Interpret:** The image is formed beyond \\(2F_2\\). Since the actual light rays intersect, the image is real and inverted. Comparing its size to the object, the image is enlarged.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 3": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "A convex lens is used to project a sharp, real, and inverted image of a candle flame onto a screen. If the size of the image on the screen is exactly equal to the size of the candle flame, determine the position of the candle flame relative to the lens. What happens to the nature and size of the image if the candle is moved slightly towards the lens?", "Solution": "1. **Given:** The lens is convex, the image is real, inverted, and of the *same size* as the object.\n2. **Determine initial position:** According to the rules of image formation by a convex lens, a real image of the same size is only formed when the object is placed exactly at twice the focal length (\\(2F_1\\)). In this case, the image is formed at \\(2F_2\\) on the other side. Therefore, the candle flame is placed at \\(2F_1\\).\n3. **Analyze movement:** If the candle is moved slightly towards the lens, its new position will be between \\(2F_1\\) and \\(F_1\\).\n4. **Determine new image characteristics:** When an object is placed between \\(2F_1\\) and \\(F_1\\), the image is formed beyond \\(2F_2\\). The new image will still be real and inverted, but it will now be *enlarged* (larger than the object).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}, "Subconcept: Image Formation in Lenses Using Ray Diagrams": {"Summary": {"Theory": "To understand how lenses form images, we can represent the process using **ray diagrams**. Just like with spherical mirrors, an infinite number of light rays originate from every point on an object. However, to locate the image of a point object, we only need to find the intersection of at least two refracted rays. For the sake of clarity, we choose two specific rays whose paths after refraction are easy to predict.\n\nHere are the three standard rules used for drawing ray diagrams for spherical lenses:\n\n1. **Ray parallel to the principal axis:** A ray of light from the object, parallel to the principal axis, after refraction from a convex lens, passes through the **principal focus** on the other side of the lens. In the case of a concave lens, the ray appears to diverge from the principal focus located on the same side of the lens. This is illustrated in the figure below.\n\n2. **Ray passing through the principal focus:** A ray of light passing through a principal focus, after refraction from a convex lens, will emerge parallel to the principal axis. Similarly, a ray of light appearing to meet at the principal focus of a concave lens, after refraction, will emerge parallel to the principal axis. This is shown in the figure below.\n\n3. **Ray passing through the optical centre:** A ray of light passing through the **optical centre** of a lens will emerge without any deviation. This holds true for both convex and concave lenses, as illustrated in the figure below.\n\nBy carefully drawing any two of these rays from a point on the object, the point where the refracted rays intersect (or appear to intersect) gives the position of the image.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C3.S6_1", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "the figure showing the first rule of ray diagrams for both convex and concave lenses.", "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "According to the rules for drawing ray diagrams, which of the following statements correctly describes the path of a light ray passing through the optical centre of a spherical lens?\n(A) It bends towards the principal focus after refraction.\n(B) It emerges parallel to the principal axis after refraction.\n(C) It emerges without any deviation.\n(D) It reflects back along its original path.", "Answer": "C", "Solution": "According to the third rule of ray diagrams for lenses, a ray of light passing through the optical centre of a lens (both convex and concave) will emerge without suffering any deviation.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A ray of light is directed such that it appears to meet at the principal focus of a concave lens. How will this ray travel after refraction through the lens?\n(A) It will pass through the optical centre of the lens.\n(B) It will emerge parallel to the principal axis.\n(C) It will diverge away from the principal axis.\n(D) It will retrace its path back towards the source.", "Answer": "B", "Solution": "Based on the second rule for drawing ray diagrams, a ray of light appearing to meet at the principal focus of a concave lens will emerge parallel to the principal axis after refraction.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "statement_selection", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Consider the following statements about drawing ray diagrams for lenses:\n1. A ray parallel to the principal axis of a concave lens appears to diverge from the principal focus located on the same side of the lens.\n2. A ray passing through the principal focus of a convex lens emerges parallel to the principal axis after refraction.\n3. A ray passing through the optical centre of a lens emerges without any deviation.\nWhich combination of statements is correct?\n(A) 1 and 2\n(B) 2 and 3\n(C) 1 and 3\n(D) 1, 2 and 3", "Answer": "D", "Solution": "All three statements are correct standard rules for drawing ray diagrams. Statement 1 describes the first rule for a concave lens. Statement 2 describes the second rule for a convex lens. Statement 3 describes the third rule applicable to both types of lenses.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "diagram", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Describe the path of a light ray from an object, which is parallel to the principal axis, after it undergoes refraction through (i) a convex lens and (ii) a concave lens.", "Answer": "(i) It passes through the principal focus on the other side. (ii) It appears to diverge from the principal focus on the same side.", "Solution": "According to the rules for ray diagrams:\n(i) For a convex lens, a ray parallel to the principal axis converges and passes through the principal focus located on the opposite side of the lens.\n(ii) For a concave lens, a ray parallel to the principal axis diverges. When extended backwards, it appears to diverge from the principal focus located on the same side of the lens as the incident ray.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "diagram", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "To locate the image of an object placed in front of a convex lens, a student chooses two specific rays: one parallel to the principal axis and another passing through the optical centre. Describe the paths of these two rays after refraction and explain how they help locate the image.", "Answer": "The parallel ray passes through the principal focus, and the ray through the optical centre goes undeviated. Their intersection point locates the image.", "Solution": "1. The first ray, which is parallel to the principal axis, will pass through the principal focus on the other side of the convex lens after refraction.\n2. The second ray, which passes through the optical centre of the lens, will emerge straight without any deviation.\n3. The exact point where these two refracted rays intersect gives the position of the image for that specific point on the object.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "diagram", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "Observe the ray diagrams shown in the figure below. State the specific rule of refraction being demonstrated for both the convex and concave lenses in this figure.", "Answer": "A ray passing through (or appearing to meet at) the principal focus emerges parallel to the principal axis after refraction.", "Solution": "The figure the figure below demonstrates the second rule of ray diagrams:\nFor the convex lens (diagram a), it shows that a ray of light passing through the principal focus, after refraction, emerges parallel to the principal axis.\nFor the concave lens (diagram b), it shows that a ray of light appearing to meet at the principal focus, after refraction, also emerges parallel to the principal axis.", "VisualContent": {"needed": true, "source_image_ref": "IMG_C3.S6_2", "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": "the figure showing rays passing through/directed at the focus emerging parallel to the principal axis.", "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 4": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "While an infinite number of rays originate from each point of an extended object, why is it convenient to consider only two specific rays for constructing ray diagrams to locate the image formed by a lens?", "Answer": "It is convenient because the paths of these specific rays after refraction are easily known, and the intersection of just two rays is sufficient to locate the image.", "Solution": "An extended object acts as a collection of point sources, each emitting infinite rays. However, to find where an image forms, we only need to find where the refracted rays intersect. By choosing two specific rays (like one parallel to the principal axis and one passing through the optical centre), we can easily predict their exact directions after refraction using standard rules. The intersection of these two easily traceable rays accurately provides the position of the image, making the diagram clear and simple to construct.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 5": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "One-half of a convex lens is covered with a black paper. Will this lens produce a complete image of the object? Verify your answer experimentally. Explain your observations.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A light ray passing through the principal focus of a spherical lens emerges parallel to the principal axis after refraction. Identify the type of lens and explain the rule governing this behavior.", "Solution": "1. **Identify the lens:** The lens is a convex lens.\n2. **Explain the rule:** According to the rules for drawing ray diagrams, a ray of light that passes through the principal focus of a convex lens will bend during refraction such that it emerges parallel to the principal axis. (Note: For a concave lens, the ray does not pass through the focus; rather, it is *directed towards* or *appears to meet* at the focus before emerging parallel).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An incident ray is directed towards the optical centre of a thin spherical lens. Describe its path after passing through the lens. Does this behavior differ between a convex and a concave lens?", "Solution": "1. **Path description:** When an incident ray is directed towards the optical centre of a thin spherical lens, it passes straight through the lens and emerges without suffering any deviation.\n2. **Comparison between lenses:** This behavior does not differ between the two types of lenses. The rule applies equally to both convex and concave lenses; in both cases, the ray passing through the optical centre remains undeviated.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 3": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "An object is placed in front of a concave lens. Using the standard rules for ray diagrams, explain step-by-step how to conceptually locate the position of the top of the image using two specific rays.", "Solution": "To locate the image of the top point of the object:\n1. **Choose Ray 1:** Draw a ray from the top of the object parallel to the principal axis. After refraction through the concave lens, this ray diverges. Draw a dotted line extending this refracted ray backwards so it appears to come from the principal focus on the same side as the object.\n2. **Choose Ray 2:** Draw a second ray from the top of the object passing directly through the optical centre of the lens. This ray will emerge straight without any deviation.\n3. **Locate Intersection:** Look at the two refracted rays. They are diverging and will not meet on the other side of the lens. However, the backward extension of Ray 1 (the dotted line) will intersect the actual path of Ray 2 on the same side as the object.\n4. **Identify Image Position:** The point where these two lines intersect is the position of the virtual image of the top of the object.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}, "Subconcept: Sign Convention for Spherical Lenses + Lens Formula and Magnification": {"Summary": {"Theory": "When dealing with spherical lenses, we follow a specific sign convention similar to the one used for spherical mirrors. All measurements are taken from the **optical centre** of the lens. According to this convention, the focal length of a convex lens is positive, and that of a concave lens is negative. You must carefully apply appropriate signs for object distance \\( u \\), image distance \\( v \\), focal length \\( f \\), object height \\( h \\), and image height \\( h' \\).\n\nThe relationship between object distance, image distance, and focal length for any spherical lens is given by the **lens formula**:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f} \\]\n\n**Magnification** (\\( m \\)) produced by a lens is the ratio of the height of the image to the height of the object:\n\\[ m = \\frac{h'}{h} \\]\n\nMagnification is also related to the object distance and image distance:\n\\[ m = \\frac{v}{u} \\]\n\nA positive sign for magnification indicates that the image is virtual and erect, while a negative sign indicates that the image is real and inverted.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A spherical lens produces a magnification of \\( m = +0.5 \\). Based on this information, which of the following statements is correct regarding the lens and the image formed?\n(A) It is a convex lens forming a real and diminished image.\n(B) It is a concave lens forming a virtual and diminished image.\n(C) It is a convex lens forming a virtual and enlarged image.\n(D) It is a concave lens forming a real and enlarged image.", "Answer": "B", "Solution": "A positive magnification (\\( m = +0.5 \\)) indicates that the image is virtual and erect. The magnitude of magnification is less than 1 (\\( 0.5 < 1 \\)), which means the image is diminished. A convex lens forms virtual images only when they are enlarged (\\( m > 1 \\)). A concave lens always forms virtual, erect, and diminished images. Therefore, it must be a concave lens.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "numerical", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An object is placed at a distance of \\( 12 \\text{ cm} \\) from a convex lens of focal length \\( 18 \\text{ cm} \\). Calculate the position of the image and its magnification.", "Answer": "\\( v = -36 \\text{ cm} \\), \\( m = +3 \\)", "Solution": "1. Given:\nObject distance, \\( u = -12 \\text{ cm} \\) (by sign convention)\nFocal length, \\( f = +18 \\text{ cm} \\) (convex lens)\n\n2. Formula:\nLens formula: \\( \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f} \\)\n\n3. Substitution:\n\\( \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} + \\frac{1}{u} \\)\n\\( \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{18} + \\frac{1}{-12} \\)\n\\( \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{2}{36} - \\frac{3}{36} = -\\frac{1}{36} \\)\n\\( v = -36 \\text{ cm} \\)\n\n4. Magnification:\n\\( m = \\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{-36}{-12} = +3 \\)\n\n5. Interpretation:\nThe image is formed \\( 36 \\text{ cm} \\) from the optical centre on the same side as the object. The positive magnification indicates the image is virtual, erect, and 3 times enlarged.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "numerical", "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "A lens produces a real, inverted image that is twice the size of the object. If the image is formed at a distance of \\( 40 \\text{ cm} \\) from the optical centre, calculate the object distance and the focal length of the lens.", "Answer": "\\( u = -20 \\text{ cm} \\), \\( f = +13.33 \\text{ cm} \\)", "Solution": "1. Given:\nThe image is real and inverted, so magnification \\( m = -2 \\).\nThe image is real, so it forms on the other side of the lens: \\( v = +40 \\text{ cm} \\).\n\n2. Find object distance (\\( u \\)):\n\\( m = \\frac{v}{u} \\)\n\\( -2 = \\frac{40}{u} \\)\n\\( u = -20 \\text{ cm} \\)\n\n3. Find focal length (\\( f \\)):\nUsing the lens formula: \\( \\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} \\)\n\\( \\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{40} - \\frac{1}{-20} \\)\n\\( \\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{40} + \\frac{2}{40} = \\frac{3}{40} \\)\n\\( f = \\frac{40}{3} \\approx +13.33 \\text{ cm} \\)\n\n4. Interpretation:\nThe object is placed \\( 20 \\text{ cm} \\) in front of the lens. The positive focal length confirms it is a convex lens.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "answer_format": "descriptive", "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "According to the New Cartesian Sign Convention, what are the signs of the focal lengths for convex and concave lenses? Furthermore, how does the sign of magnification help in determining the nature of the image formed by a lens?", "Answer": "The focal length of a convex lens is positive, and that of a concave lens is negative. A positive magnification indicates a virtual and erect image, while a negative magnification indicates a real and inverted image.", "Solution": "By convention, all measurements are taken from the optical centre. The principal focus of a convex lens lies on the right side (direction of incident light), making its focal length positive. The principal focus of a concave lens lies on the left side, making its focal length negative. For magnification (\\( m = h'/h \\)), the object height \\( h \\) is always positive. If the image is erect (virtual), \\( h' \\) is positive, making \\( m \\) positive. If the image is inverted (real), \\( h' \\) is negative, making \\( m \\) negative.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Question 4": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A spherical mirror and a thin spherical lens have each a focal length of -15 cm . The mirror and the lens are likely to be", "Answer": "a", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 5": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An object 5 cm in length is held 25 cm away from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm . Draw the ray diagram and find the position, size and the nature of the image formed.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 6": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A concave lens of focal length 15 cm forms an image 10 cm from the lens. How far is the object placed from the lens? Draw the ray diagram.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 7": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A convex lens forms a real and inverted image of a needle at a distance of 50 cm from it. Where is the needle placed in front of the convex lens if the image is equal to the size of the object? Also, find the power of the lens.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A concave lens of focal length \\( 20 \\text{ cm} \\) forms an image \\( 15 \\text{ cm} \\) from the lens. If the height of the image is \\( 3 \\text{ cm} \\), find the distance of the object from the lens and the height of the object.", "Solution": "1. Given:\nA concave lens always forms a virtual, erect image on the same side as the object.\nImage distance, \\( v = -15 \\text{ cm} \\)\nFocal length, \\( f = -20 \\text{ cm} \\) (concave lens)\nHeight of the image, \\( h' = +3 \\text{ cm} \\) (erect image)\n\n2. Find object distance (\\( u \\)):\nUsing the lens formula: \\( \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f} \\)\nRearranging for \\( \\frac{1}{u} \\):\n\\( \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{f} \\)\n\\( \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{-15} - \\frac{1}{-20} \\)\n\\( \\frac{1}{u} = -\\frac{1}{15} + \\frac{1}{20} \\)\n\\( \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{-4 + 3}{60} = -\\frac{1}{60} \\)\n\\( u = -60 \\text{ cm} \\)\n\n3. Find object height (\\( h \\)):\nMagnification, \\( m = \\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{h'}{h} \\)\n\\( \\frac{-15}{-60} = \\frac{3}{h} \\)\n\\( \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{h} \\)\n\\( h = +12 \\text{ cm} \\)\n\n4. Interpretation:\nThe object is placed \\( 60 \\text{ cm} \\) in front of the lens, and its height is \\( 12 \\text{ cm} \\).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": true, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A concave lens has focal length of 15 cm. At what distance should the object from the lens be placed so that it forms an image at 10 cm from the lens? Also, find the magnification produced by the lens.", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "ncert"}, "Example 3": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": true, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "A 2.0 cm tall object is placed perpendicular to the principal axis of a convex lens of focal length 10 cm. The distance of the object from the lens is 15 cm. Find the nature, position and size of the image. Also, find the magnification.", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "ncert"}}}, "Subconcept: Power of a Lens": {"Summary": {"Theory": "The ability of a lens to converge or diverge light rays depends on its focal length. A convex lens with a short focal length bends light rays through larger angles, focusing them closer to the optical centre. Similarly, a concave lens with a short focal length causes higher divergence than one with a longer focal length. The degree of convergence or divergence achieved by a lens is expressed in terms of its **power**.\n\nThe power \\( P \\) of a lens is defined as the reciprocal of its focal length \\( f \\):\n\\[ P = \\frac{1}{f} \\]\n\nThe SI unit of power is the **dioptre** (denoted by the letter D). \n- 1 dioptre is the power of a lens whose focal length is 1 metre (\\( 1 \\text{ D} = 1 \\text{ m}^{-1} \\)).\n- To calculate power in dioptres, the focal length *must* always be expressed in metres.\n- By sign convention, the power of a convex lens is positive (e.g., \\( +2.0 \\text{ D} \\)), and the power of a concave lens is negative (e.g., \\( -2.5 \\text{ D} \\)).\n\nMany optical instruments, such as cameras, microscopes, and telescopes, use a combination of lenses to increase magnification and image sharpness. When multiple lenses are placed in contact, their net power \\( P \\) is simply the algebraic sum of their individual powers:\n\\[ P = P_1 + P_2 + P_3 + \\dots \\]\nThis simple additive property makes it very convenient for opticians to design corrective lens combinations during eye testing.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}}, "SCQs": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": false, "scq_format": "standard", "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "An optician places two lenses of power \\( +3.5 \\text{ D} \\) and \\( -1.5 \\text{ D} \\) in contact inside a testing frame. What is the focal length of the equivalent single lens formed by this combination?\n(A) \\( +50 \\text{ cm} \\)\n(B) \\( +20 \\text{ cm} \\)\n(C) \\( +0.5 \\text{ cm} \\)\n(D) \\( -50 \\text{ cm} \\)", "Answer": "A", "Solution": "1. The net power \\( P \\) of lenses placed in contact is the algebraic sum of their individual powers: \\( P = P_1 + P_2 \\).\n2. Given \\( P_1 = +3.5 \\text{ D} \\) and \\( P_2 = -1.5 \\text{ D} \\).\n3. Net power \\( P = +3.5 + (-1.5) = +2.0 \\text{ D} \\).\n4. The focal length \\( f \\) is the reciprocal of power: \\( f = \\frac{1}{P} \\).\n5. \\( f = \\frac{1}{+2.0} = +0.5 \\text{ m} \\).\n6. Converting to centimetres: \\( +0.5 \\text{ m} = +50 \\text{ cm} \\). Therefore, the correct option is (A).", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}, "SubjectiveTypes": {"Question 1": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Medium", "Question": "Which of the following lenses would you prefer to use while reading small letters found in a dictionary?", "Answer": "Concave lens; -1.25 D", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 2": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Find the focal length of a lens of power -2.0 D . What type of lens is this?", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 3": {"isBackExercise": true, "isInTextQuestion": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "A doctor has prescribed a corrective lens of power +1.5 D . Find the focal length of the lens. Is the prescribed lens diverging or converging?", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "back_exercise"}, "Question 4": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Define 1 dioptre of power of a lens.", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}, "Question 5": {"isBackExercise": false, "isInTextQuestion": true, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Find the power of a concave lens of focal length 2 m .", "Answer": "", "Solution": "", "VisualContent": {"needed": false}, "_source": "in_text"}}, "Examples": {"Example 1": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Easy", "Question": "Calculate the power of a convex lens whose focal length is \\( 20 \\text{ cm} \\).", "Solution": "1. **Given:** Focal length of the convex lens, \\( f = +20 \\text{ cm} \\). (By sign convention, the focal length of a convex lens is taken as positive).\n2. **Find:** Power of the lens, \\( P \\).\n3. **Formula:** \\( P = \\frac{1}{f} \\), where \\( f \\) must be in metres.\n4. **Substitute:** First, convert the focal length to metres: \\( f = \\frac{+20}{100} \\text{ m} = +0.20 \\text{ m} \\). Now, substitute this value into the formula: \\( P = \\frac{1}{+0.20} \\).\n5. **Answer:** \\( P = +5.0 \\text{ D} \\).\n6. **Interpret:** The power of the lens is \\( +5.0 \\text{ dioptres} \\). The positive sign confirms that it is a converging (convex) lens.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}, "Example 2": {"isSolvedExample": true, "isFromNCERT": false, "Difficulty": "Hard", "Question": "A lens system consists of two lenses placed in contact. The first lens is a concave lens of focal length \\( 50 \\text{ cm} \\). If the net power of the combination is \\( +1.0 \\text{ D} \\), find the power and focal length of the second lens.", "Solution": "1. **Given:** Focal length of the first lens (concave), \\( f_1 = -50 \\text{ cm} = -0.5 \\text{ m} \\). Net power of the combination, \\( P = +1.0 \\text{ D} \\).\n2. **Find:** Power of the second lens, \\( P_2 \\), and its focal length, \\( f_2 \\).\n3. **Formula:** Power of a single lens \\( P = \\frac{1}{f} \\) (with \\( f \\) in metres). Net power of lenses in contact \\( P = P_1 + P_2 \\).\n4. **Substitute:** \n   - Calculate the power of the first lens: \\( P_1 = \\frac{1}{-0.5} = -2.0 \\text{ D} \\).\n   - Use the net power formula to find \\( P_2 \\): \\( +1.0 = -2.0 + P_2 \\).\n   - \\( P_2 = +1.0 + 2.0 = +3.0 \\text{ D} \\).\n   - Calculate the focal length of the second lens: \\( f_2 = \\frac{1}{P_2} = \\frac{1}{+3.0} \\text{ m} \\).\n5. **Answer:** \\( P_2 = +3.0 \\text{ D} \\) and \\( f_2 = +0.33 \\text{ m} \\) (or \\( +33.3 \\text{ cm} \\)).\n6. **Interpret:** The second lens has a power of \\( +3.0 \\text{ D} \\). Since its power and focal length are positive, it is a convex (converging) lens.", "VisualContent": {"needed": false, "source_image_ref": null, "image_generation_needed": false, "image_description": null, "generation_description": null}, "_source": "generated"}}}}};
